## My conclusion

**I would expand your app from “find a shaded route” into “find the best way, place, and time to be outside.”**

That gives you substantially more room to develop the product without turning it into a collection of unrelated navigation features.

I also would not conclude that your app is failing simply because its feature list sounds short. Navigation, saved routes, and an assistant could be a complete product. The more important question is whether someone can accomplish a useful, recurring task without filling the gaps elsewhere.

Your current description primarily answers:

> “I know where I’m going. How do I get there with less sun?”

The most promising adjacent questions are:

> “When should I leave?”
> “I have 30 minutes—what could I do outside?”
> “Where will it be pleasant **when I arrive and while I’m there**?”
> “What should I do when my plan changes?”

Those are the directions I would investigate—not simply adding more transportation modes.

The research below is current to **September 5, 2026**. I found your public repository and reviewed its product documentation, but this is market and product research, **not a hands-on audit of your deployed app**. Your existing growth roadmap already contains several sensible ideas; the aim here is to identify which deserve priority rather than repeat it as a new checklist. 

---

## 1. The market is more consumer-oriented than it first appears

### Shadowmap is not exclusively for businesses

Shadowmap currently offers a free tier, an Explorer tier for uses including photography and outdoor planning, a Home tier, and professional Studio offerings. Its **Sunny Seats** concept also connects outdoor seating with sun and shade conditions. Your impression of professional emphasis is understandable, but “they serve businesses; I serve regular people” is not enough of a distinction. ([Shadowmap][1])

Also, **Shadowmap**, **ShadeMap.app**, and your **ShadeMapNav** are separate projects.

The most relevant competitive picture is:

| Product                   | What it offers or advertises                                                                                              | What it means for you                                                                                                                              |
| ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **ShadeMap.app**          | Shadow exploration and GPX route replay.                                                                                  | Solar visualization and route inspection already have alternatives. ([Shademap][2])                                                                |
| **Shadehopper**           | Consumer shade routing, route alternatives, loops, departure guidance, multiple activities, and shaded-side instructions. | A close competitor—not a professional analysis tool. These are advertised capabilities, not independently verified performance. ([Google Play][3]) |
| **Undercover, Singapore** | Shortest, shadiest, and balanced pedestrian routes. Its official February 2026 page labels it a limited release.          | A locally excellent pedestrian experience is a credible approach. ([Hack for Public Good][4])                                                      |
| **ASU Cool Routes**       | Weather- and geometry-aware thermal routing, currently described at Tempe-campus scale.                                   | Scientifically richer routing is possible, but scaling and computation remain substantive challenges. ([ASU News][5])                              |
| **Cool Walk / ShadeWalk** | Web-based shade-routing concepts, including time-aware progression or loop generation.                                    | Basic shade routing is becoming easier to reproduce. ([Cool Walk][6])                                                                              |

There is another important competitor: **the navigation app people already use**.

Google’s August 2026 Ask Maps update reinforces that conversational local discovery and trip-related planning are already incumbent capabilities. A generic “plan me a fun day” assistant will therefore be difficult to distinguish through conversation alone. ([blog.google][7])

**The opportunity is not merely having an agent or calculating shadows. It is connecting those capabilities into a better decision than the alternatives provide.**

There is an important uncertainty, though: I did **not** find credible independent market-size or retention figures for standalone consumer shade navigation. Existing products demonstrate that people are working on the problem—not that a large, durable consumer market has already been established.

---

## 2. What people actually appear to want

I found stronger evidence for underlying navigation needs than for a precise ranking of “features people want in a shade app.” That distinction matters.

### People value shade, but they still care about time and effort

A Singapore experiment analyzed 408 sun-condition route choices from 46 participants. Its model estimated an average perceived cost of walking in sun around **1.16 times** equivalent shaded distance. That supports shade-sensitive route choice, but it does **not** establish that everyone will accept a particular detour. ([Nature][8])

**Implication:** “Find me more shade without adding more than five minutes” is a better product instruction than “maximize shade.”

### People often cannot change their trip very much

A 2025 observational study in Pacoima, Los Angeles County, found relatively little change in high-use pedestrian locations during extreme heat, especially on weekdays. The researchers discuss constraints on changing routines and emphasize putting shade where people already travel. ([Nature][9])

**Implication:** distinguish **“I must leave now”** from **“my timing is flexible.”** An app that recommends waiting until evening is not solving a fixed afternoon appointment.

### For cycling, route suitability cannot be secondary

PeopleForBikes’ public summary of a 16,000-adult survey reports that **53% of riders worried about being hit by a car**, while only **50% were familiar with local bike lanes, paths, and trails**. ([PeopleForBikes][10])

**Implication:** shade should be optimized among appropriate routes. It should not compensate for an unsuitable road, dangerous crossing, or surface the rider cannot comfortably use.

### More navigation machinery is not always better

A March 2026 study found that **44% of its 222 survey respondents preferred route preview**, rising to **76% in familiar environments**. This supports giving pedestrians an understandable overview and control—not assuming everyone wants constant driving-style instructions. ([PLOS][11])

An earlier Singapore sheltered-walking prototype also reported useful testing lessons around starting orientation and landmark photographs. ([Hack for Public Good][12])

**Implication:** clear entrances, recognizable landmarks, and an inspectable route can be more valuable than another elaborate map layer.

### Amenities can determine whether an outing works

An age-friendly neighborhood project highlighted seating, toilets, crossings, and connectivity as important to older residents. That is population-specific evidence, but it illustrates why a route line alone may not complete the job. ([Walk Wheel Cycle Trust][13])

**Implication:** a verified shaded seat or usable toilet can matter more than shaving another small amount of exposure from a route.

Taken together, my interpretation is:

> **People need a manageable, understandable, suitable trip—not the mathematically shadiest line regardless of everything else.**

---

## 3. The three features I would prioritize

### A. “When should I leave?”

**This is the most natural extension of your existing engine.**

A time slider makes the user manually investigate changing shade. A departure comparison does that work for them.

For example:

> “I need to arrive by 3:00. When should I leave to minimize direct sun without adding more than five minutes of walking?”

The result should compare **route and departure time together**, showing arrival time, walking duration, estimated direct-sun minutes, and the longest continuous exposed stretch.

An illustrative comparison—not real route data—might read:

> **Leave now:** 18-minute walk, approximately 9 minutes in direct sun.
> **Leave in 30 minutes:** 19-minute walk, approximately 5 minutes in direct sun.
> **Take the longer alternative:** 22-minute walk, approximately 3 minutes in direct sun.

That is much more actionable than “this route is 76% shaded.”

It also gives saved routes a purpose beyond bookmarking: **recalculate my familiar trip for today**, rather than preserve a historical shade score.

The smallest useful version could compare a bounded set of departure times over a short window. You do not need an exhaustive optimizer immediately.

One important detail: **total sun time matters alongside percentage**. A 30-minute route that is 70% shaded contains nine minutes outside shade; a 20-minute route that is 60% shaded contains eight. A larger percentage does not automatically mean less exposure.

---

### B. “I have 30 minutes. Take me somewhere pleasant.”

**This is the strongest near-term evolution of your “fun day in the shade” concept.**

A full-day itinerary is one use case. A short outing is a different, potentially repeatable job:

> “Give me a 30-minute walk from here, mostly shaded, with somewhere to refill water, and bring me back.”

The result should be an actual navigable outing—not a paragraph naming several places.

I would start with **20-, 30-, and 45-minute options**, a simple activity preference, and one optional stop. Offer understandable variations such as “more shade,” “simpler route,” or “better destination.”

Crucially, the total budget includes stopping. A 30-minute outing should not secretly mean 30 minutes of movement plus another 25 minutes at suggested stops.

Useful controls would be:

* **Shorten this** or get me home.
* **Add one stop**, such as water, a seat, or somewhere free.
* **Keep it easy**, with constraints appropriate to the selected mode.

An out-and-back should be allowed when it is better than a contrived loop. Avoid zigzags and repeated crossings just to achieve a high shade score.

My hypothesis is that **small, repeatable outdoor decisions may be more valuable for retention than elaborate occasional itineraries**. That needs testing, but it is a coherent next experiment.

---

### C. “Where will it be good when I get there?”

**This is the most interesting expansion beyond navigation.**

Instead of recommending a destination in isolation, recommend a **place and a suitable time window**.

Possible requests include:

> “Find a bench where I can read in shade for the next hour.”

> “Which nearby playground will have useful shade during a 45-minute visit?”

> “Where can we sit outside with some sun, but not direct sun the whole time?”

> “Find a viewpoint worth reaching before sunset, and plan the return.”

The critical distinction is:

**Not:** “This park is shady now.”

**Instead:** “This specific seating area is expected to be shaded when you arrive and remain so during your visit.”

That means evaluating four connected things:

**The route there → conditions at arrival → conditions during the stay → the return journey.**

A park centroid or a café’s business address is not necessarily the correct location for this calculation. You may need the seating area, terrace, playground section, or exact viewpoint.

I would start with **20–50 curated places in one area**, not promise this everywhere. Record useful entrances, access restrictions, hours, amenities, photographs where permitted, and verification dates.

This is not an untouched market: Shadowmap’s Sunny Seats already overlaps with sun-aware outdoor seating. Your opportunity would be making the **whole visit** easy to plan, not claiming exclusivity over sunlight-based discovery. ([Shadowmap][14])

A particularly promising extension is **search by exposure budget**:

> “Show me places reachable with roughly five minutes or less in direct sun.”

That is meaningfully different from a conventional distance radius.

---

## 4. Transit is valuable—but its distinctive feature should be the whole exposed journey

I agree that transit belongs in the product. I would challenge the idea that it is **obviously always the best way to avoid sun**, however.

For product purposes, compare:

> **Access walk + exposed waiting + transfers + egress walk.**

An uncovered stop with a long wait may be worse than a short shaded walk. A different station entrance might remove an exposed plaza. A delayed bus could change the preferred option.

Citymapper already markets its **Walk Less** option partly around heat, humidity, and rain. Simply reducing walking is therefore not a unique proposition. Your more specific contribution is explaining **where exposure remains and when it happens**. ([Citymapper][15])

I would develop transit in stages:

**First:** shade-aware access and egress, the correct entrance, useful departure timing, and handoff to an established transit planner where necessary.

**Then:** complete itineraries in one city, including waiting and transfers.

GTFS can represent station entrances and internal pathways, while realtime trip updates can inform waiting estimates. But a specification does not guarantee complete local data, and it does not automatically tell you whether a bus stop is shaded. ([General Transit Feed Specification][16])

The distinguishing question should be:

> “Which door-to-door option gives me the outdoor experience I want?”

—not merely “Can the app display a bus leg?”

---

## 5. Other additions that fit—and which I would defer

| Feature direction               | Why it belongs                                                                                   | My priority                                                           |
| ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Comfort stops and bailout**   | Find usable water, seating, toilets, an indoor break, or a way home without abandoning the trip. | **Early**, with verified availability.                                |
| **Saved trips that update**     | Reassess shade for the current departure and season rather than preserve stale results.          | **Early**, alongside departure comparison.                            |
| **Mode-specific suitability**   | Walking, cycling, skating, and stroller use should change constraints—not just speed.            | **Foundational** for modes you advertise.                             |
| **Meet somewhere fairly**       | Choose a meeting place balancing each person’s travel time and exposure.                         | **Later experiment**, after timed destinations work.                  |
| **Share a timed outing**        | Let a friend open the plan and recompute it for their departure.                                 | **Useful adjacent feature**, without building a social network.       |
| **Prefer sun on cooler days**   | Reuses the same time-and-light foundation for a different preference.                            | **Seasonality experiment**.                                           |
| **Rain-sheltered routing**      | Addresses a related outdoor-comfort problem.                                                     | **Later**: roofed shelter and shadow coverage are different datasets. |
| **Photorealistic future views** | Potentially appealing, but dependent on an already useful place recommendation.                  | **Defer**.                                                            |

### Boarding and skating could be a deliberate niche

OpenSkateMap’s pavement-quality mapping—including explicit “unknown” quality—is a useful signal that rolling users need information beyond a standard bike route. It is not evidence of a large addressable market by itself. ([OpenSkateMap][17])

A credible rolling mode would consider surface quality, steps, curb transitions, slope, and crossings. **“Smooth and shaded” could be a compelling focused proposition**, but the difficult part is trustworthy local data.

I would either test that deliberately with local riders or clearly label limited support. Changing the bicycle icon to a skateboard would not complete the job.

---

## 6. Your agent should execute plans, not merely describe them

The strongest version of your assistant is a conversational interface to capabilities your app can verify.

For each stop, it should resolve a concrete location, arrival and dwell window, relevant access information, route legs, and an exposure estimate. The same plan should be inspectable on the map.

Good next commands would include:

> “I’m leaving 20 minutes late—adjust this.”

> “Swap the café for somewhere free.”

> “Keep the sunset stop, but remove the steep hill.”

> “Find somewhere shaded to sit for the next hour.”

> “I’m tired. Get me home with less walking.”

These are useful because they modify an actual trip.

The model should not invent path legality, exact shade, opening hours, or departures. Those should come from structured tools and data. When an important fact is unknown, that uncertainty should survive into the answer.

### Your sunset-picture idea is worthwhile, with one important distinction

Separate **a real reference photograph**, **a modeled solar-position overlay**, and **a generated illustration**.

They are different products. A convincing generated image is not evidence that a viewpoint exists or a forecast of future cloud color.

PhotoPills already offers sun- and time-based photographic planning. Your opportunity could be a much more approachable experience: **discover the spot, understand when to arrive, get there comfortably, and get back**. ([PhotoPills][18])

I would start with a real, appropriately licensed photograph, the exact viewpoint and direction, the relevant time window, and a return plan. Add generated previews later, clearly labeled as illustrations.

**The recommendation should remain valuable even without the generated picture.**

---

## 7. What I would build next, in practice

I would organize the next version around three entry points:

**Go somewhere. Go outside. Find a good spot.**

Then proceed through four stages.

**First, make the existing route decision trustworthy.** Show meaningful time/exposure tradeoffs, advance the shade calculation along the expected journey, make unknown data visible, and provide a simple way to report mistakes. I cannot tell from this research whether those are current weaknesses; they are prerequisites to test.

**Second, add departure comparisons and short outings.** These most directly reuse your current capabilities and give people reasons to return.

**Third, test curated timed destinations.** This is where the app can become more than a navigation utility without losing its identity.

**Fourth, deepen transit where actual journeys justify it.** Prioritize exposed waiting, entrances, and transfers rather than global transit parity.

Throughout, keep **shade, thermal comfort, UV protection, and rain shelter** distinct. The National Weather Service’s heat-index guidance is based on shady conditions, and WHO explicitly notes that shade does not provide complete UV protection. Avoid presenting a geometrically shaded route as medically “safe.” ([National Weather Service][19])

For validation, I would start with exploratory interviews and walk-alongs with roughly 15–20 people in one well-mapped area. That is a qualitative pilot, not a representative survey. Observe real route choices, whether recommendations change decisions, whether predicted conditions match reality, and whether people voluntarily return on another relevant day.

The metric I would care about is **repeat successful outdoor trips**, not feature usage in isolation or time spent admiring the map.

**Ultimately, your app does not need to do everything a navigation app could do. It needs to become particularly good at a decision other apps make people assemble themselves: where to go, when to go, and how to make the whole outdoor experience work.**

