## My recommendation

**Build ShadeMapNav into a time-aware outdoor planner that can create a feasible itinerary, explain its tradeoffs, and repair it when the day changes.**

Then make its engineering inspectable: show how you model changing shadows, verify an agent’s actions, measure prediction accuracy, and recover from failures.

The combination I would prioritize is:

> **Sun Budget + Living Itinerary + a public evaluation suite.**
> For a stronger ML-engineering portfolio, add a small, rigorously evaluated **real-world shade-learning system**.

Imagine this interaction:

> “I have 90 minutes. Find coffee and somewhere shaded to read for 20 minutes. Keep my estimated time in direct sun below eight minutes, and get me back before 5:30.”

The app displays the actual route, arrival times, reading window, exposure estimates, and uncertainties.

Then:

> “I’m leaving 20 minutes late. Keep the reading stop, but replace the café with somewhere free.”

It repairs the itinerary, explains what changed, and verifies the revised constraints.

**That is the product I would find more compelling than “a navigation app with an AI assistant.”** It also gives you substantive work in algorithms, spatial databases, agent execution, evaluation, and potentially ML—all serving the same experience.

