## 5. For ML engineering: **Reality Check**

### The product idea

> **“The map thinks this sidewalk is shaded. Reality disagrees. Help it learn.”**

Let users report incorrect shade, missing canopy, or an overlooked awning. The app identifies uncertain areas and improves its predictions from independently collected observations.

This is my preferred ML extension because it creates a real target outside the model itself: **observed outdoor conditions**.

### The implementation I would pursue

Start with the geometry engine as your baseline. Collect a small, carefully documented observation dataset in one neighborhood.

Then compare a simple learned correction—such as logistic regression or gradient-boosted trees—with the geometry-only baseline. Inputs could include solar position, street orientation, geometry coverage, available canopy information, and observation conditions.

Only add a computer-vision model when the data and failure analysis justify it. A useful extension could segment canopy or other occluders and feed that information into your physical model.

There is an important data distinction here: USGS describes lidar point clouds that capture structures and vegetation, while bare-earth elevation products remove those features. A terrain elevation model alone is not a building-and-tree shadow model. ([USGS][12])

### The rigorous part is what makes it impressive

I would require geographic and temporal holdouts—not random adjacent frames from the same walk appearing in training and test sets.

Track false-shade predictions, route-level exposure error, and whether the learned component actually changes route decisions beneficially. For probabilistic predictions, examine reliability diagrams as well as scoring metrics; scikit-learn’s documentation cautions that a lower Brier score alone does not establish better calibration. ([Scikit-learn][13])

Also, do not confuse cloudy conditions, darker camera exposure, and geometric shade. Define the label carefully.

This would demonstrate data collection, labeling, model selection, leakage prevention, calibration, deployment, and failure analysis—the kinds of capabilities visible in the geospatial ML role I reviewed. ([Greenhouse][7])

**The impressive outcome is not necessarily a large neural network. It is a credible improvement on held-out reality.**

---

## 6. For systems engineering: **City Capsules**

An alternative specialization is:

> **“Download this neighborhood. Keep planning when the connection disappears.”**

Not just an installable PWA: an actual offline package containing the graph, needed geometry or exposure representation, selected places, and permitted map assets.

I would build versioned snapshots, integrity checks, interrupted-download recovery, atomic updates, stale-data warnings, and a clear boundary between offline features and online-only services.

PMTiles could help with map-tile archives, but it is not itself a routing graph or a complete offline policy. ([Protomaps Docs][14])

Run computation behind a worker boundary. Profile the TypeScript implementation before deciding whether a Rust/WASM kernel is justified.

The demonstration is simple and compelling: download an area, disable networking, change the departure time, calculate a route, restart, and resume.

Publish package size, memory, cold/warm latency, and result agreement on documented devices. That is a substantial systems project without pretending you have millions of users.

