## 7. Three “cool and funky” additions that fit the same system

I would pick **one** of these after the core works.

### **Golden-Hour Rendezvous**

Two friends start in different places. The app finds somewhere pleasant to meet at the right time while balancing each person’s travel and exposure.

The technical problem becomes multi-origin optimization and fairness: minimizing average inconvenience might leave one person with a terrible journey, whereas minimizing the worst individual burden produces a different answer.

This also incorporates your sunset idea naturally: an exact viewpoint, appropriate arrival window, reference image, and return plan.

### **Shadow Chase**

A short outing becomes a challenge to follow shade while visiting interesting places. The order changes because the shadows move.

It makes the time-dependent planner immediately understandable and playful. Keep it on lawful, suitable paths; do not incentivize rushing or unsafe crossings.

### **The Shadow Lab**

Let someone place a hypothetical canopy or tree-sized object and watch routes and modeled exposure change.

This could be a beautiful graphics/geometry demonstration: “What would one extra shade structure do here?”

Label the assumptions. A projected shadow is not a validated urban-cooling simulation.

**These are proposed experiments, not claims of globally unique inventions. Their value is that they make your underlying engineering visible.**

