## 9. What I would actually build, in order

**First, establish a trustworthy baseline.** Confirm the source findings against a pinned commit, run the existing checks, fix the time/provider issues, and finish or remove visible placeholder actions.

**Second, build Sun Budget in one bounded area.** Make traversal-time exposure and explicit constraints work against small reference cases.

**Third, build Living Itinerary and ShadeBench together.** Introduce completed-result tools, versioned plans, deterministic validation, cancellation, and one excellent repair interaction.

**Fourth, choose a specialization.** For MLE, build the observation dataset and Reality Check. For systems/backend, build City Capsules or a measured worker-processing platform. Do not start all of them simultaneously.

Finally, make the evidence easy to find. The inspected README is brief; I would lead it with the working demo, architecture, one important technical decision, reproducible results, and limitations. 

The portfolio claim you should aim to earn is something like:

> “I built a time-dependent outdoor planner, integrated an agent that produces and repairs validated itineraries, and measured its correctness, latency, cost, and real-world prediction error.”

Then support each part with an artifact someone can inspect.

**Your app does not need more technologies to become interesting. It needs one unusually good capability whose difficult parts you can explain and prove you handled well. For your goals, the strongest candidate is a planner that understands when you will be somewhere—not just where you are going—and an agent that remains accountable to that plan.**

